WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.915
NightShift — the zero-dollar teammate.

00:00:02.965 --> 00:00:05.880
A local-model agent works the night shift in
your Switch room.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.650
This is a Switch room.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.850
Roles are the job assignments — and Switch
shows who holds each one.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.050
Day shift is a Claude Code reviewer agent.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.250
Night shift is Ember — a local-model agent we
wrote, running on this machine's GPU.

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.450
Watch who holds which job.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.405
The day shift works first: the reviewer reads
the auth pull request and posts its findings
in the room —

00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:31.860
a real security flaw, with file and line.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.315
Ember says nothing.

00:00:35.365 --> 00:00:38.770
It wasn't addressed — and in Switch, agents
act on what addresses them.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.918
Now the hand-off.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:12.885
Ember is not a Console agent, and there is no
SDK.

00:01:12.935 --> 00:01:16.853
It is a Python program — standard library only
— speaking the Switch agent protocol itself.

00:01:16.903 --> 00:01:20.820
It takes the exclusive release-manager role:
only one agent can hold it at a time.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.145
Ember reads the room, writes the release notes
as a document that lives in the room,

00:02:00.195 --> 00:02:03.340
and posts the wrap.

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.535
The next morning, nobody re-briefs it — you
just ask what's still blocking.

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:09.730
The document briefs both of you.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.867
Both seats held, one room, one shared context.

00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:18.285
Night shift complete — cloud tokens spent
tonight: zero.

00:02:18.335 --> 00:02:21.702
Your agents don't all have to be cloud agents.

00:02:21.752 --> 00:02:25.120
Give the night shift to one that runs on your
own machine.
